WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.102
Day 7 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.652 --> 00:00:09.473
Harnesses 101: test harness, eval harness and agent harness.

00:00:10.023 --> 00:00:14.727
The three harnesses that coexist in QA today, what each one wraps and how to spot

00:00:14.727 --> 00:00:16.004
what your stack lacks.

00:00:17.304 --> 00:00:21.846
The word "harness" shows up in almost every conversation about AI and testing, and

00:00:21.846 --> 00:00:24.006
almost always with a different meaning.

00:00:24.306 --> 00:00:26.990
For some it's the usual automation framework.

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:30.033
For others, the tool that evaluates a prompt.

00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:35.189
For others, the program that turns a model into an agent able to edit code.

00:00:35.489 --> 00:00:40.151
All three exist and all three share the name, and that confusion makes teams buy or

00:00:40.151 --> 00:00:41.668
build what they don't need.

00:00:42.968 --> 00:00:47.148
This guide separates the three meanings with a single common definition and shows

00:00:47.148 --> 00:00:47.974
how they relate.

00:00:48.274 --> 00:00:53.034
There is no code today: the goal is for you to look at your own stack and say which

00:00:53.034 --> 00:00:57.507
harness you have, which one you lack and, above all, which one verifies which.

00:00:58.807 --> 00:01:00.505
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:01.055 --> 00:01:03.101
Know your current automation stack.

00:01:03.651 --> 00:01:06.347
The list below, to fill in with your tools.

00:01:07.647 --> 00:01:09.043
The three harnesses.

00:01:09.593 --> 00:01:11.650
Test harness: wraps your app.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.762
Runner, fixtures, mocks and reporters.

00:01:15.062 --> 00:01:19.082
Examples: Playwright Test, JUnit, pytest.

00:01:19.632 --> 00:01:22.491
Eval harness: wraps a model or prompt.

00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:25.568
Dataset, runner, scorers and report.

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:30.016
Examples: promptfoo, DeepEval, Inspect AI.

00:01:30.566 --> 00:01:33.645
Agent harness: wraps a model that acts.

00:01:33.945 --> 00:01:37.442
Loop, tools, context, permissions and memory.

00:01:37.742 --> 00:01:41.506
Examples: Claude Code, Copilot agent, Cursor.

00:01:42.806 --> 00:01:43.842
Step 1.

00:01:44.142 --> 00:01:45.608
A single definition.

00:01:46.158 --> 00:01:50.561
Keep the definition: a harness is everything around the thing under test that lets

00:01:50.561 --> 00:01:52.279
you run, control and measure it.

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:57.151
A harness is everything around the thing under test that lets you run, control and

00:01:57.151 --> 00:01:57.731
measure it.

00:01:58.031 --> 00:02:00.983
Running it means starting it in a repeatable way.

00:02:01.283 --> 00:02:04.548
Controlling it, fixing its inputs and its environment.

00:02:04.848 --> 00:02:07.729
Measuring it, getting a comparable result.

00:02:08.029 --> 00:02:12.692
The definition works for all three types; the only thing that changes is what sits

00:02:12.692 --> 00:02:15.706
in the middle: your application, a model or an agent.

00:02:16.006 --> 00:02:20.656
When someone uses the word, ask what's in the middle and the conversation sorts

00:02:20.656 --> 00:02:21.303
itself out.

00:02:22.603 --> 00:02:23.802
Step 2.

00:02:24.102 --> 00:02:26.774
Test harness: the one you already have.

00:02:27.324 --> 00:02:29.626
Test harness: you already have it.

00:02:29.926 --> 00:02:33.586
Runner, fixtures, mocks and reports around your app.

00:02:34.136 --> 00:02:37.215
If you run automated tests, you already have one.

00:02:37.515 --> 00:02:42.803
The runner executes, fixtures prepare state, mocks isolate dependencies and reports

00:02:42.803 --> 00:02:43.822
show the result.

00:02:44.122 --> 00:02:48.577
It's deterministic: the same input gives the same output and a failure means

00:02:48.577 --> 00:02:49.163
something.

00:02:49.463 --> 00:02:53.820
It's worth recognizing as a harness because it's the base for the other two.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:58.809
A team with a slow, brittle test harness that nobody looks at will carry those same

00:02:58.809 --> 00:03:01.634
problems into everything it builds with AI on top.

00:03:02.934 --> 00:03:04.016
Step 3.

00:03:04.316 --> 00:03:06.908
Eval harness: the same around a model.

00:03:07.458 --> 00:03:11.234
Eval harness: the same idea around a model or prompt.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:14.845
Asserts become scorers and cases become a dataset.

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:18.149
Here the thing under test is a model or a prompt.

00:03:18.449 --> 00:03:23.182
The pieces have direct equivalents: test cases become a dataset, asserts become

00:03:23.182 --> 00:03:28.034
scorers that grade an output, and the report shows pass rates instead of green or

00:03:28.034 --> 00:03:28.274
red.

00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:33.330
Two things are added that the test harness didn't need: repeating each case several

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.450
times and recording cost and latency.

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:39.282
You'll need it the day a feature of your product depends on a model.

00:03:40.582 --> 00:03:41.595
Step 4.

00:03:41.895 --> 00:03:44.347
Agent harness: the one that works.

00:03:44.897 --> 00:03:50.381
Agent harness: the loop that turns a model into an agent, with tools, instructions,

00:03:50.381 --> 00:03:51.900
permissions and memory.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.154
Here the agent does QA work.

00:03:54.704 --> 00:03:59.827
It's the loop that turns a model into an agent: it receives a task, decides which

00:03:59.827 --> 00:04:03.623
tool to use, reads the result and decides again, until done.

00:04:03.923 --> 00:04:09.127
Around it are repository instructions, available tools, permissions and memory.

00:04:09.427 --> 00:04:13.963
The key difference from the other two is the role: this one doesn't measure, it

00:04:13.963 --> 00:04:14.480
produces.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:18.788
It writes tests, modifies code, investigates a failure.

00:04:19.088 --> 00:04:23.426
That's why configuring it feels more like onboarding a new teammate than setting up

00:04:23.426 --> 00:04:23.792
a tool.

00:04:25.092 --> 00:04:26.221
Step 5.

00:04:26.521 --> 00:04:27.905
Who verifies whom.

00:04:28.455 --> 00:04:33.728
Key relationship: the agent harness produces tests and changes; the test harness and

00:04:33.728 --> 00:04:36.051
eval harness verify what it produced.

00:04:36.351 --> 00:04:37.770
Never the other way round.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:41.109
The relationship runs in one direction only.

00:04:41.409 --> 00:04:46.568
The agent harness produces tests and changes; the test harness and the eval harness

00:04:46.568 --> 00:04:48.122
verify what was produced.

00:04:48.422 --> 00:04:53.234
If you reverse the arrow and let the agent judge its own work, you lose independent

00:04:53.234 --> 00:04:55.844
verification, which is the whole point of QA.

00:04:56.144 --> 00:05:00.648
In practice this becomes a concrete rule: what counts as done is the result of a

00:05:00.648 --> 00:05:05.377
command running outside the agent, with its exit code, and not the summary the agent

00:05:05.377 --> 00:05:05.771
writes.

00:05:07.071 --> 00:05:08.258
Step 6.

00:05:08.558 --> 00:05:10.093
Your stack on the list.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:13.885
Fill the list with your stack and flag the harness you lack.

00:05:14.185 --> 00:05:15.627
That's your next step.

00:05:16.177 --> 00:05:19.221
Fill in the list of three harnesses with real names.

00:05:19.521 --> 00:05:22.670
Which runner, fixtures and reports you use.

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:26.862
If you evaluate a model, with which dataset and scorers.

00:05:27.162 --> 00:05:31.263
If you use an agent, which rules, tools and permissions it has.

00:05:31.563 --> 00:05:33.621
Empty cells are information.

00:05:33.921 --> 00:05:38.749
If you lack the eval harness and your product already uses a model, that's the next

00:05:38.749 --> 00:05:40.611
step, and it's tomorrow's topic.

00:05:40.911 --> 00:05:46.161
If you lack the agent one, it can wait: it's productivity, not verification.

00:05:47.461 --> 00:05:48.567
An example.

00:05:49.117 --> 00:05:51.221
A product team fills in the list.

00:05:51.521 --> 00:05:57.003
Test harness: Playwright, login fixtures, HTML report in CI; complete.

00:05:57.303 --> 00:06:02.378
Eval harness: empty, even though the product has had an assistant answering customer

00:06:02.378 --> 00:06:03.889
queries for three months.

00:06:04.189 --> 00:06:08.661
Agent harness: two people use a coding agent with no shared rules.

00:06:08.961 --> 00:06:10.729
The diagnosis is clear.

00:06:11.029 --> 00:06:15.510
The riskiest feature, the assistant, has no measurement at all, while the team was

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:17.150
debating which agent to adopt.

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:21.635
They decide to build a twenty-case eval for the assistant first and then write a

00:06:21.635 --> 00:06:23.362
common rules file for the agents.

00:06:24.662 --> 00:06:26.325
The most common mistakes.

00:06:26.875 --> 00:06:29.815
Using "harness" without saying what sits in the middle.

00:06:30.365 --> 00:06:33.096
Letting the agent declare its own work done.

00:06:33.646 --> 00:06:37.143
Investing in an agent harness while the test harness is brittle.

00:06:37.693 --> 00:06:41.631
Evaluating a model with exact asserts instead of an eval harness.

00:06:42.931 --> 00:06:45.175
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:45.725 --> 00:06:49.106
You can explain the three harnesses with the same definition.

00:06:49.656 --> 00:06:53.583
Your list has concrete names in each cell or an explicit gap.

00:06:54.133 --> 00:06:57.444
You identified which one you lack and why it matters.

00:06:57.994 --> 00:07:01.329
You have the rule written down that the gate runs outside the agent.

00:07:02.629 --> 00:07:04.524
And where AI fell short.

00:07:05.074 --> 00:07:09.477
Without an external gate, the agent reports success without having run anything.

00:07:09.777 --> 00:07:12.879
Verify with the exit code, not the summary.

00:07:14.179 --> 00:07:15.529
That was day 7.

00:07:15.829 --> 00:07:20.803
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:20.803 --> 00:07:21.810
asked questions.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:26.229
Continue with day 8: Your first eval harness with promptfoo.
